  '137': {
    'eTerm': 'Sepsis',
    'eDesc': 'Sepsis is like, how when you get hurt, and your body rushes a bunch of protective fluid to where the injury is. The blood vessels expand and the area gets swollen, so that your body can repair the area. Sepsis is when an infection triggers the impulse to protect the body and causes swelling everywhere - all of your blood vessels expand. It can get out of control fast. Blood pressure drops and your vital signs go out of wack, which, without treatment, can cause your kidneys, brain, and liver, to be deprived of essential blood flow. Without treatment, organ failure and death can occur.',
    'yTerm': 'Science / Pathophysiology:\nSepsis is characterized by an infection that enters the blood stream and causes vital sign changes, like: high temperature, low blood pressure, high heart rate, high respiratory rate / rapid breathing. It is usually also accompanied by abnormal lab findings, in particular high or very low white blood cell count. \n\nCommon Treatments / Care:\nThere are protocols for sepsis, including:\n\n-Draw Blood Cultures & other labs\n-Secure IV access\n-Give fluid IV\n-Give antibiotics\n-Treat abnormal vital signs (ie, if they\'re feverish, give tylenol / ice packs, get their blood pressure up, give oxygen if they\'re struggling to breathe)\n\nIf the person continues to get worse, treatment can include:\n-Pressors\n\nRelevant Definitions:\n""-IV: intravenous. A tiny tube that goes into the vein, that allows us to give medications and sometimes draw blood\n-Antibiotics: medications that fight infections\n-""""Fluid"""": specific formulas of saltwater that we give through IV\'s to treat lots of different kinds of medical issues / keep someone hydrated\n-Pressors: Also called Vasopressors, refers to a class of drugs that squeezes the blood vessels so as to increase blood pressure. There are several medications in this category, but are generally just referred to as pressors outside of detailed communication between healthcare providers""',
    'yDesc': '1. Kilinrem siissia augmun iterluni alingnarqellriaruluni tuquciyugngaluni puvqercirituli.                   2.  Naullutem ciissian puvqercetellra augem avayai.',
    'speakers': ['Sepsis-aaqwatenayuquq', 'akngirtaqaavet', "temvet-llumer'iquyurrlutengkitugingnaqlukuakngiruuten.Augemnutngassutaiqerrurlutengavatii-llupuvqercelluku", 'temevetassiringcarniaraiavatii.Kilinremayagniraqakumamtengnaqlukutemapuvivkarlukutemiitamalkuan.Tamarmeng-llutememavayaipuvqerrluteng.Arcarillracukayugngauq.Augem-llunutngaarallraaciqsigiluni.Qailluqerrluni-lluyuuciq', 'aulukumanrilkata', 'yungcaumanrilkunitartuq', 'pateq', 'tenguk-llunuqlitengciqutaugmek.Yungcaumanrilkuneng', 'cakunglluutcalinrirciquttuqunaqluteng-llu.'],
    'resourceID': '13'
  },
  '138': {
    'eTerm': 'Pneumonia',
    'eDesc': "An infection of the lungs. It can interfere with oxygen getting into the blood and make it hard to breathe. It can cause organ failure if it either becomes a systemic infection (for ex, sepsis), or if oxygen is unable to get into the blood to the point that organs aren't getting oxygenated (for ex, COVID pneumonia). ",
    'yTerm': 'Science / Pathophysiology:\nIn pneumonia, the areas of the lung that typically allow air to enter the body and interface with the blood stream become filled with phlegm and/or fluid. Pneumonia causes shortness of breath, changes seen on X-ray imaging, cough, congestion, and potentially other vital sign changes / changes in the person\'s disposition (ie, fatigue / unresponsiveness / organ failure).\n\nCommon Treatments / Care:\nOxygen\nAntibiotics (IV or oral/pills)\nSupportive Care\nIntubation: in which the doctor puts a tube into someone\'s mouth & trachea, to create an airway through which we can control someone\'s breathing using an ambu-bag or ventilator. During this time the person will receive tube feeds for nutrition\nVentilator: a machine that controls breathing\nIf the person is unable to get off the vent / be extubated, then they might be trach\'d and peg\'d\nTracheostomy: a hole in the throat that gives direct access to trachea, so that someone\'s breaths can be delivered directly by medical professionals long term\nTrach often goes with """"Peg"""", or G-Tube / Gastrostomy tube, which is a tube into the stomach so the person can receive nutrition\n\nRelevant Definitions:\n""""Oral"""": by mouth?? So if we\'re saying """"oral medication,""" we mean a medication that\'s taken by mouth, or like, in a different context, if we say """"oral hygeine,""" we mean the hygiene of the mouth\n-Supportive Care: Really means anything we do that supports the specific situation of the body of the individual in front of us. Can mean: pain management, fluids, ventilators, pressors, etc\n-Tube feeds: a liquid mixture that we put through a feeding pump so people can get calories\n-Vent: same as ventilator\n-Extubation: taking the breathing tube out\n',
    'yDesc': 'Patsaqaq',
    'speakers': ["Pugtautemkumlaqertellra.Iterciigalivkaryugngalukuoxygen-aaqaugmunanerniqnarivkarluku.Cakunglluutcalinrircecugngalukinaulluutngureskan(sepsis-aatun)wall'oxygen-aqiterciigalikanaugmuncakunglluut-llucalisciigalivkarlukioxygen-ailata(COVID-aaggun", 'patsaqercaraq).'],
    'resourceID': '13'
  },
  '139': {
    'eTerm': 'Renal Failure / Kidney Failure / Renal Disease',
    'eDesc': "The kidneys get rid of water, so if they're not working, fluid backs up throughout the body. The person stops peeing. The extra burden of fluid can cause swelling everywhere (esp in sepsis) and the toxins and electrolytes that the kidneys normally get rid of stay in the body. So, labs can get super out of wack, and if the person doesn't get these things out of their body, they can die. We monitor electrolytes frequently in order to make sure the lab values don't get too wacky.",
    'yTerm': 'Science / Pathophysiology:\nCan happen because of an acute event, like sepsis - the blood pressure is so low that the kidneys don\'t get blood flow, and stop working - or from chronic disease, like diabetes - the persistently elevated blood sugar damages the blood vessels\n\nCommon Treatments / Care:\n-Diuresis: an intervention to help kickstart the kidneys into working. Diuretics (""water pills"") help get rid of fluid and, as fluid is pulled out of the body, electrolytes & other toxins go with it\n-Dialysis\n\nRelevant Definitions:\n-Diuresis: producing urine. Pills & IV medications can help produce urine\n-Dialysis: a machine that mimics the function of the kidney. There are several kinds of dialysis, including hemodialysis, which people can do outpatient, generally 3 x a week, and can also do if there\'s an acute event & they\'re in the hospital (ie, overdose), and CRRT - a continuous form of dialysis that can only happen in the ICU. Dialysis is run by nurses and a nephrologist\n',
    'yDesc': 'Tartuq Calinrirluni/ Tartuq Calinrirluni/ Tartuum Naulluutii',
    'speakers': ["Tartummer'ayagciquralaaraa", "tua-i-llucalinrilkunekmer'ungalnguutayagviirrlutengtememi.Yuk-lluqurrsuirulluni.Mer'ungalnguuttememipuvivkaryugngaatem'iitamalkuan(arcarqerlukunaullluutemsiissiaaugmunitraqan-sepsis)calituqunarqellriityuunqegcessuucet-llu?(electrolytes)ayagcetetukaittartumuitaurlutengtememi.Augillritayuquciimegteggunayuqenrirluteng", 'tamakutantenrilkatkiyuumtemiinek', 'tuquyugngalunitauna.Kelluquralaraputkesianekelectrolyte-aitaugitpaqtellritayuquciicetunuitatassiirturluki.'],
    'resourceID': '13'
  },
  '140': {
    'eTerm': 'Congestive Heart Failure (CHF)',
    'eDesc': "Every time your heart beats, it pushes blood throughout your body. Heart failure is when the mucles of the heart aren't able to push hard enough to get blood through; this causes blood to back up. ",
    'yTerm': "Science / Pathophysiology:\nCHF is a chronic process over time & has significant impact on people's everyday lives, esp r/t fatigue, shortness of breath, and reduction in activity. People with CHF are often so tired and out of breath that it becomes hard for them to tolerate any kind of activity. When the heart isn't pumping fully, it can cause fluid to back up into the lungs (which is what causes shortness of breath) and/or legs, causing leg swelling. \n\nCommon Treatments / Care:\n-Diuresis, to get rid of the burden of fluid on the heart\n-Fluid restriction\n-Medications to help the heart pump stronger\n-Supportive Care: meds to keep blood pressure in range, help out kidneys if they're having trouble, oxygen if lungs are struggling, etc\n\n",
    'yDesc': 'Ircaquq ellminek nutngaurarciigaliluni (CHF)',
    'speakers': ['Ircaqunnutngaaqami', 'augmekayagceciilartuqtemevnun.Ircaqumnutngaurallratukniarutngaqaamiyualuiircaquumcaknaanarituuqanciurallraaugmek;waten-llupiaqamiauknatvarviirutlartuq.'],
    'resourceID': '13'
  },
  '141': {
    'eTerm': 'Withdrawal (Alcohol withdrawal, can also happen with street drugs, and can also happen because of the sedating medications we give in the ICU)',
    'eDesc': "When someone's body isn't getting the substance it's used to, they can become very delirius and agitated. For alcohol, from day 3- day 10 without alcohol is the potentially life-threatening period. We treat their symptoms of withdrawal based on how severe symptoms are.",
    'yTerm': "Science / Pathophysiology:\nThe receptors that alcohol & other drugs attached to are now free, which causes cravings and physical withdrawal symptoms. Most hospitals use a scale like this one to measure withdrawal symptoms. The higher the score, the more likely it is the person might have life-threatening symptoms, such as seizures. \n\nCommon Treatments / Care:\nWe give medications based on the symptoms - so if someone is having very severe withdrawal, they might need so much sedation that they can't keep their mouth open / they're not awake enough to cough. But, if their withdrawal is mild, then we only have ot give them small doses, and they might be otherwise appropriate. Treatment for withdrawal is all considered supportive care; it really depends on the situation.",
    'yDesc': "Aturturarraarluni camek atuyuirtellran ayuqucia temem elisngakiinek unangyuirucaaraam ayuqucia (revisit) (Taangarturatulim temii taanganritqallran ayuqucia temii taangamek atunrillran    iinrurrluut ilakluki, cali qavarcetaat/qinuirucetaat iinruitnek piicesciigacugngaluni, iinrukit'laqngamtenek ICU-mi)",
    'speakers': ['Kiayuumtemiiunangenritaaqancamekelisngakiinektemiin', "usviirtellriatunayuqliriilartuqeqnayukaryuka'ariiluni-llu.Taanganritaqamiyuk3-10ernerniyuucimunalingnarqellriarulartuq.Yungcalararputtaqesciigalutaitangvaglukiqaillunalingnarqucii."],
    'resourceID': '13'
  }
